This is the GLOBAL interest owed by ALL borrowers.
When a user pays interest, `burntInterestValue` is subtracted from `_unrealizedGlobalInterest`.
If a user's historical interest decreases because their `netBorrows` decreased while `userBorrowIndex` was not updated, the protocol expects them to pay less interest than they actually owed.
But the global `_unrealizedGlobalInterest` already includes the interest they originally owed!
So `_unrealizedGlobalInterest` will be LARGER than the sum of all users' individual `interestOwed`.
Is this a problem?
`_unrealizedGlobalInterest` is used in `totalAssets()`:
```solidity
    function totalAssets() public view returns (uint256) {
        unchecked {
            return uint256(s_depositedAssets) + s_assetsInAMM + s_marketState.unrealizedInterest();
        }
    }
```
If `_unrealizedGlobalInterest` is larger than the actual interest that can be collected from users, `totalAssets()` is inflated.
This means the share price (`totalAssets() / totalSupply()`) is inflated.
When users withdraw, they get more assets per share than they should.
Eventually, the vault might not have enough assets to pay everyone, leading to a shortfall (bad debt).
